    "innovation": "This paper introduces a novel spatio-temporal jump model for clustering urban environmental data, a framework that handles missing values and identifies meaningful thermal comfort regimes by incorporating persistence across both spatial and temporal dimensions.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "spatio-temporal jump model (ST-JM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Balanced Accuracy",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "For the ST-JM model, we evaluate \u03bb and \u03b3 values on a grid from 0 to 0.25, with increments of 0.05 for each simulated dataset. The optimal hyperparameters are selected based on maximizing the balanced accuracy (BAC) between true and estimated state sequences, which is defined as...",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "k-prototype algorithm",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Balanced Accuracy",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "We compare our proposed method to the k-prototype (k-prot) algorithm by Huang (1998), which can be seen as a special case of ST-JM with \u03bb = 0 and \u03b3 = 0, that is, a standard k-means algorithm extended to handle mixed-type data. For more details on this method, see Szepannek et al. (2024).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "spatio-temporal jump model (ST-JM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "accuracy",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "We test predictive ability of the ST-JM comparing our clustering with thermal comfort feedbacks collected from users at different locations and times. This dataset consists of 54 responses to the question, \u201cThermally, what do you prefer now?\u201d with options \"Warmer\", \"No change\", and \"Cooler\u201d. Each feedback is matched with the median cluster from the closest weather station within a 4.92 km radius of the user's location at the time of feedback submission. We select this distance as the minimum required to ensure that every feedback has at least one corresponding weather station cluster for comparison. The model achieves an accuracy of 74%, which is an impressive outcome considering that no physiological variables were used and no personal information on respondents, such as age, sex, and body mass index is available.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "k-prototype model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "accuracy",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "For comparison, we also fitted a k-prototype model on the same dataset and evaluated against the reported feedbacks, obtaining an accuracy of 54%.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "UTCI",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "We also include UTCI (\u00b0C) data obtained from the \u201cthermal comfort indices derived from ERA5 reanalysis\u201d database (Napoli et al. 2020).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SOLWEIG model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "UTCI",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The latter limitation could be addressed by using the SOLWEIG model (Lindberg et al. 2008) to provide spatially detailed UTCI estimates and incorporate variables such as Sky View Factor (SVF) and Green View Factor (GVF).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
